            <ArticleTitle>[Increase in bispectral index induced by antihyperalgesic dose of ketamine].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>513-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report two cases of sudden increase in Bispectral Index (BIS) after the injection of low-dose ketamine for the prevention of postoperative hyperalgesia. The two patients were anaesthetised with a continuous infusion of remifentanil associated with propofol for one and isoflurane for the other. Changes in BIS occurred while the two patients were in a stable phase of surgery (beginning of parietal closure and suture of an anastomosis) and had a stable target concentration of anaesthetic agents. No others signs of awakening were observed. The BIS value returned progressively to 40-50 despite no increase in target concentration. None of the patients complained of intra-operative recall.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
